  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nApproaching warm front and upper level shortwave from the west\nwill bring increasing coverage of showers this evening and\novernight, with some embedded thunderstorms developing after\nmidnight and continuing through Tuesday morning. PWAT's \nincrease overnight to 1.75 to just under 2 inches, along with\nforcing from approaching upper level shortwave as well as\ndeveloping dual upper level jet structure (one upper jet across\nnorthern Maine, another approaching from Great Lakes region)\nshould allow for some heavier bands of showers to develop later\ntonight. Some weak elevated instability could also allow for a\nfew embedded thunderstorms. Rainfall rates of 0.50-1\"/hour could\noccur.\n\nIt appears that heaviest rain potential might be near or north\nof I-90 around daybreak Tuesday where best forcing overlaps deep\nmoisture, before shifting south and east during Tuesday \nmorning. Will have to watch for ponding of water in low \nlying/poor drainage and urban areas for the Tuesday morning \ncommute, with isolated flash flooding possible. \nShowers/thunderstorms should then shift south of the region by \nmidday with areas of drizzle then lingering.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAdditional showers/thunderstorms are expected Tuesday afternoon\nas some surface based instability develops, mainly for areas \nsouth and west of Albany. 12Z/HREFs suggest MU CAPES of \n1000-2000 J/kg may develop in these areas by late Tuesday \nafternoon, amid 0-6 km shear of 30-40 KT, with greatest overlap \nacross the SE Catskills and mid Hudson Valley, which is where \nthe Slight Risk for severe thunderstorms has been included for \nTuesday afternoon. Latest CAMs suggest some potential for\nsupercell development across the mid Hudson Valley region\nTuesday afternoon, with isolated to scattered instances of large\nhail and damaging winds possible. Given enhanced low level\nshear across portions of the mid Hudson Valley and NW CT in\nclose proximity to an effective warm frontal boundary (possibly\nreinforced from earlier convective outflow), can not rule out a\ntornado or two in the mid Hudson Valley region as well.\nShowers/thunderstorms may eventually shift south and east of the\nregion Tuesday evening as forcing and instability decrease\nfurther.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nStronger upper level shortwave approaches from the Great Lakes,\nalong with surface occluded front late Wednesday into Thursday.\nAlthough much of Wednesday may just be cloudy with only spotty\nshowers or drizzle and rather cool temperatures in the 60s/lower\n70s, areas of rain and embedded thunderstorms are expected to\ndevelop Wednesday evening and continue into Thursday. Main\nconcern during this time period will be additional locally heavy\nrain, especially should it coincide spatially with areas which\nreceive antecedent heavy rainfall. An area of Slight Risk for\nExcessive Rainfall will be located across the Mohawk Valley, SW\nAdirondacks and upper Hudson Valley region with a Marginal Risk\nfor the remainder of the region. Surface based instability may\nremain limited to the westernmost Mohawk Valley, eastern\nCatskills and perhaps mid Hudson Valley, however elevated\ninstability should allow for a few embedded thunderstorms,\nespecially areas west of the Hudson River Valley. Should any\nbreaks in the clouds occur Wednesday through Thursday, a few\nstronger thunderstorms can not be ruled out, especially across\nportions of the Mohawk Valley, mid Hudson Valley and NW CT given\nthe strong mid level winds (40-50 KT) expected across the area.",
